  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 AM MDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nOne of the big things that meteorologists look for in forecasting is \npatterns. And we have had a pattern over most of the past 5 weeks. \nAnd that is that the hottest temperatures occurred on weekends. And \nthis one looks to be no exception, although maybe by only a few \ndegrees.\n\nThe hottest temperatures will likely be today. Model guidance \ncontinues to show 700 millibar temperatures climbing over 20 degrees \ncelsius. And this translates to some portions of the area likely \nclimbing to over 100 degrees this afternoon. Most locations under \n5400 feet have at least a 1 in 2 chance of reaching 100 this \nafternoon, including the Wind River Basin, the eastern Big Horn \nBasin as well as near Casper. Helping to increase the temperature \nwill be a downsloping southwesterly breeze. And this, combined with \nthe hot temperatures and humidity falling into the single digits, \nwill bring the likelihood of critical fire weather. We currently \nhave a Red Flag Warning in effect for entire area where fuels are \ncritical. Wind is marginally in some of the northern zones of the \narea. However, with the very dry fuels and low humidity, we will let \nit go for now. And, it still looks to be a dry day. Mid level \nmoisture will increase a bit in southwestern Wyoming, and may be \njust enough for a few virga showers to develop. The chance is only \naround 10 percent at any given spot and with that we left the POPs \nlargely silent. And, like the past few days, we will have areas\nof smoke around that will bring unhealthy air quality at times.\n\nThe operative word for Sunday will be slightly, as in conditions \nshould change, but only slightly. As for the heat, slightly cooler \n700 millibar temperatures (about a degree celsius), should also for \nslightly cooler temperatures, but only a couple of degrees. And, \nrelative humidity will be slightly higher. However, only by a few \npercent and low enough when combined with the continuing breezy \nconditions, will likely bring more critical fire weather in the \nafternoon. And finally, with slightly more instability and slightly \nmore mid level moisture, the chance of a shower or even \nthunderstorms will go up slightly in southern and western Wyoming. \nThe chance is still at most 1 out of 6 and any shower or storm would \nlikely only bring wind gusts and not any needed rain. And, smoke\nmay be slightly better, but air quality will likely remain\nunhealthy.\n\nAs for next week, it looks like a transition week. Monday and \nTuesday still look like rather hot days, with most areas East of the \nDivide seeing high temperatures in the 90s. A bit more monsoonal \nmoisture may try to work in and bring a few showers and \nthunderstorms. However, on this day, most would be confined to \nwestern Wyoming and still fairly sparse, with generally a less than \n1 out of 4 chance. The ridge should then weaken and move to the \nsouth and east, bring a more zonal flow. This could allow for more \nmoisture to reach the area and bring an increased chance of \nconvection. There is still a lot of disagreement in guidance with \ndetails, so determining a more active day or placement of the most \nnumerous storms is still very difficult at this time. And, \ntemperatures should trend down starting on Wednesday and especially \nfor Thursday and into next weekend when a cold front may pass and \nbring in some below normal temperatures for the end of next week and \ninto next weekend. However, until we reach that, it looks like more \nof the same for the next four days. That is hot temperatures, \nelevated to critical fire weather and little to no chance of \nmeaningful rain.",
